  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing confidence in a cold snap beginning \nThursday through early next week. Very cold wind chills\npossible. \n\nAs a closed low takes a southern then easterly trajectory into\nthe Mid Atlantic towards the weekend, reinforcing cold air \nwaves will push temperatures down into the 30s and 40s for highs\nFriday and Saturday for the mountains, while the Piedmont drops\ninto the 50s for highs. Overnight lows will fall into the 20s \nand 30s for lows by Thursday night in the mountains, with strong\ncold air advecting into the Piedmont by Friday night into early\nnext week. There will be several nights of hard killing freezes\nbeginning Friday, with even the Piedmont dropping into the \nupper 20s to low 30s. \n\nThe other concern will be strong and gusty W and then NW winds\nwhich will begin Thursday and continue into the weekend. Very \ncold wind chills develop, and will be worst Friday and Saturday \nnights, where parts of the mountains will drop into the 8-15F \ndegree range for the \"feels like\" temperature. While it will be \nvery unpleasant and a shock to the system, we will be several \ndegrees too warm for any Cold Weather advisories. Temperatures \nrebound some early next week. \n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: A system brings light rain and eventually snow to\nthe mountains Thursday through Saturday night. \n\nMild WNW flow will dominate through tonight. Today and Wednesday \nlook like the last warm days in the foreseeable future. Spokes \nof upper level energy moving around an upper low will help \ntrigger surface cyclogenesis over the OH and TN Valleys and \nsteer a system into the southern and central Appalachians \nThursday. This will trigger rain showers mainly over the \nmountains but they will also be possible across portions of the \nPiedmont Thursday afternoon. \n \nPrecipitation will mix with and eventually change over to light\nsnow as we transition into a NW upslope event that will \ncontinue into Saturday, mainly impacting the Greenbrier Valley. \nAmounts will be low each day and it will be difficult for the \nsnow to stick to the warmer ground initially. But as the event \ncontinues we could see some light accumulations.",
